\begin_layout Title
Sharpe Ratio Maximisation:
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
This article is written in the UK.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

a convex reformulation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I wrote this originally for a homework question but I find it cool enough (5°) to make public.
 I might add some pictures to make it cooler (2°).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Setup
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Fix the number of risky assets 
\begin_inset Formula $n\ge1$
\end_inset

;
 we are given a set of whose elements are called 
\emph on
feasible
\emph default
 
\emph on
portfolios
\emph default
 (I think this could be relaxed to any convex set,
 but that's how it was given in the problem)
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
X=\{x∈\mathbb{R}^{n}:f_{i}(x)≤τ_{i},i=1,\ldots,m,Ax≤b\}
\]

\end_inset

 for some positive-homogenous subaditive 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

s and a bounded polyhedron 
\begin_inset Formula $Ax\le b$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We map 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
X\xrightarrow{(\mu,\sigma)}\mathbb{R}^{2}
\]

\end_inset

each risky portfolio to the point 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mu^{\top}x,\sigma(x))$
\end_inset

 of the mean and variance of its return.
 In this article we disregard the entire distribution of the portfolio returns and just focus on those two statistics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An important point on the mean-variance plane 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $(r_{0},0)$
\end_inset

,
 which corresponds to riskless portfolios of deterministic return 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 We fix a 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{0}>1$
\end_inset

,
 so the rate by which a riskless asset's value grows in one time period.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\series bold
Optimal Sharpe ratio problem
\series default
 is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{x\in X}S(x),\qquad S(x)\coloneqq\frac{\mu^{\top}x-r_{0}}{\sigma(x)}
\]

\end_inset

here 
\begin_inset Formula $S(x)$
\end_inset

 is the Sharpe ratio for the portfolio 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 which is the slope of the Capital Allocation Line through 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $S(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is not a convex function,
 so the maximisation problem above is not convex.
 We are asked to derive a convex formulation for it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We will do that by lifting the map 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 to a map 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{S}$
\end_inset

 on a cone 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{X}$
\end_inset

 generated by 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

,
 and then picking another slice of that cone,
 
\begin_inset Formula $X'\subset\hat{X}$
\end_inset

,
 over which 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{S}$
\end_inset

 is easily equivalent to a convex problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Construction of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{X}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $X'$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Generate by 
\begin_inset Formula $X\subset\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

 a cone in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$
\end_inset

 by defining
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{X}\coloneqq\left\{ (px,p)\in\mathbb{R}^{n}\times\mathbb{R}_{\ge0}:p>0\text{ and }x\in X\right\} ,
\]

\end_inset

and extend 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{S}(px,p)\coloneqq S(x)\label{eq:S extension}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

 (this is well-defined because each point in 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{X}$
\end_inset

 is uniuely expressible as 
\begin_inset Formula $(px,p)$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I imagine 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{X}$
\end_inset

 as a multiplicatively expanding cone generated by 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

,
 where each point 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in X$
\end_inset

 corresponds to a ray in 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{X}$
\end_inset

.
 If we denote the unit embedding 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon(x)\coloneqq(1\cdot x,1)$
\end_inset

 and the projection 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi(px,p)\coloneqq x$
\end_inset

,
 we get the following situation:
\end_layout

