\begin_layout Standard
The maximum Sharpe ratio problem maximises the left vertical map here;
 we will now show that it is equivalent to maximise over the right one and how it enables a convex reformulation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Convex reformulation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From the above discussion,
 the horizontal compositions in diagram 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "commdiag"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
X\xrightarrow{\pi'\circ\varepsilon}X'\text{ and }X'\xrightarrow{\pi\circ\varepsilon'}X
\]

\end_inset

 are inverses,
 so it is equivalent to optimize over 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $X'$
\end_inset

.
 But over 
\begin_inset Formula $X'$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{S}$
\end_inset

 admits the simple expression
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{S}(z,p)\overset{\eqref{eq:S reformulated}}{=}\frac{\mu^{\top}z-pr_{0}}{\sigma(z)}\overset{!}{=}\frac{1}{\sigma(z)}
\]

\end_inset

 because of the constraint 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu^{\top}z-pr_{0}=1$
\end_inset

,
 and so 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\max_{x\in X}S(x) & =\max_{(z,p)\in X'}\hat{S}(z,p)=\max_{(z,p)\in X'}\frac{1}{\sigma(z)}\\
 & =1/\min_{(z,p)\in X'}\sigma(z).
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma(z)$
\end_inset

 is convex,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(z,p)\in X'$
\end_inset

 is a convex constraint because each constitutive constraint in 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
(z,p)\in X' & \Leftrightarrow z/p\in X\text{ and }\mu z-pr_{0}=1\\
 & \Leftrightarrow\forall i:f_{i}(z/p)\le\tau_{i}\text{ and }Az/p\le b\text{ and }1=\mu z-pr_{0}
\end{align*}

\end_inset

 is convex on its own:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}(z/p)\le\tau_{i}$
\end_inset

 is convex because by 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

's positive homogeneity it is equivalent to 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}(z)\le p\tau_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 and so to 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}(z)-p\tau_{i}\le0$
\end_inset

,
 which is convex by convexity of 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

 (part of the coherence condition assumed on each 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $Az/p\le b$
\end_inset

 is just 
\begin_inset Formula $Az\le pb$
\end_inset

,
 so linear and convex;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $1=\mu z-pr_{0}$
\end_inset

 is linear,
 and so convex,
 too.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It remains to recover the optimal 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\max_{x}S(x)$
\end_inset

 from the optimal 
\begin_inset Formula $(z,p)$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\min\limits_{(z,p)}\sigma(z)$
\end_inset

.
 By construction 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:S extension"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 for any 
\begin_inset Formula $(z,p)\in\hat{X}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{S}(z,p)=S(x),\quad\text{with }px=z,
\]

\end_inset

so if a pair 
\begin_inset Formula $(z^{*},p^{*})$
\end_inset

 maximises 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{S}(z,p)$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\coloneqq z^{*}/p^{*}$
\end_inset

 maximises the Sharpe ratio 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus we showed that the non-convex optimum Sharpe ratio problem over 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is equivalent to a convex minimization one over 
\begin_inset Formula $X'$
\end_inset

 by the relation
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\max_{x\in X}S(x) & =1/\min_{(z,p)\in X'}\sigma(z).\\
x^{*} & \leftrightarrow(z^{*},p^{*})\\
\text{with }x^{*} & =z^{*}/p^{*},
\end{align*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\leftrightarrow(z^{*},p^{*})$
\end_inset

 is one-to-one because if 
\begin_inset Formula $(z,p),(z',p')\in X'$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $z/p=z'/p',$
\end_inset

 then 
\begin_inset Formula $z'=p'/p\cdot z$
\end_inset

,
 substituted in the constraint 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu^{\top}z'-p'r_{0}=1$
\end_inset

,
 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu^{\top}z-pr_{0}=p/p'$
\end_inset

 forcing 
\begin_inset Formula $p/p'=1$
\end_inset

,
 thus 
\begin_inset Formula $p=p'$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z=z'$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

